%description:

Tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment:on -- the last buffered segment carries PSH.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "set PSH on the last buffered segment, after which no queued data waits
(RFC 9293 section 3.9.1.2, MUST-61)".

The classes: on (true, the default), off (false; LegacyTcp). The code: PSH on the segment
after which no queued data waits, unless pushSegmentsOnWriteBoundary replaces the rule
(TcpConnection::sendSegment()). This test separates "on" from "off".

Host under test: host1, which sends 100 bytes. Observation: level 1, the data segment has PSH.

%# parameter: Tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment
%# class: on
%# value: true
%# key: *.host1.tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpPushLastBufferedSegmentOn")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("tcp.pshBit == true")
             .describe("the data segment of host1, with PSH")
             .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpPushLastBufferedSegmentOn compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment = true    # the parameter under test
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 100B
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpPushLastBufferedSegmentOn: PASS
